-- allium: 3
-- viewer_slot.allium
--
-- Phoenix Conversation Viewer Slot: Behavioural Specification
--
-- Scope: The single-slot, user-action-driven viewer system that overlays
--        or sits beside a conversation's chat column. Governs which
--        viewer is open (prose / diff / browser), the data each viewer
--        carries, and the mutex rules between them.
-- Includes: Open / close transitions, browser-session edge auto-open
--           and auto-close, conversation-change reset, the URL contract
--           that lets a cold reload (iOS PWA kill, refresh) restore the
--           exact viewer + data the user was looking at.
-- Excludes:
--   - Phase-driven overlays (TaskApprovalReader, QuestionPanel,
--     ErrorBanner, CredentialHelperPanel, ContextExhaustedBanner,
--     TerminalBanner). These layer ON TOP of the viewer slot per
--     bedrock's phase transitions; they are not part of the slot's
--     mutex. See bedrock.allium for their entry/exit triggers.
--   - The chat column itself, the conversation atom, and any phase
--     state. See bedrock.allium / conversation_atom.allium.
--   - Responsive layout decisions (overlay vs full-screen vs
--     split-pane based on viewport). The spec governs WHAT is open;
--     CSS + ConversationPage decide HOW it's rendered. The slot is
--     the same regardless of layout.
--   - Per-viewer presentation: ProseReader's annotation/notes
--     subsystem, DiffView's commit log rendering, BrowserViewPanel's
--     CDP wire protocol. Viewers fetch their own payloads on mount.
--   - The mobile FileBrowserOverlay (a file-picker drawer that lets
--     the user choose a file to open). It is NOT a viewer; selecting
--     a file from it transitions the slot to prose. The drawer's open
--     state is component-local UI affordance, out of scope here.
--
-- Architecture: the slot is a discriminated union -- `kind` selects
--   exactly one of {none, prose, diff, browser}, and the data each
--   variant carries lives only in the variant that needs it. This
--   makes "prose open without a file path" or "diff open without a
--   comparator" structurally unrepresentable.
--
--   The URL is the source of truth. Search params encode the slot:
--     ?viewer=prose&file=<absolute-path>&root=<absolute-path>
--     ?viewer=diff&commit=<sha>&base=<ref>
--     ?viewer=browser
--   patchContext (modifiedLines: Set<Integer>) rides alongside in
--   conversation-scoped state, not in the URL -- it is patch-
--   provenance, not view identity. Loss on cold reload is acceptable.
--
-- Requirement traceability: REQ-VS-001 through REQ-VS-014

use "../bedrock/bedrock.allium" as bedrock

-- Note: the URL search params are the persistence medium for the
-- slot; the spec governs which params correspond to which slot
-- state. Today the implementation reads/writes via react-router's
-- useSearchParams in FileExplorerProvider; this widens to the
-- unified slot provider per REQ-VS-002. URL access is described
-- in the entity comment and @guidance blocks rather than modelled
-- as a typed external entity -- the slot's state IS the URL truth,
-- so a separate Url entity would be a parallel representation.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Identity of an open file in the prose reader. `path` is absolute;
-- `root_dir` is the conversation's working directory and gates how
-- the file is resolved server-side. patchContext is intentionally
-- separate (see Entities below) -- it is provenance, not identity.
value ProseFile {
    path: String        -- non-empty, absolute
    root_dir: String    -- non-empty, absolute
}

-- Identifier for what the diff viewer is showing. The full diff
-- payload (committed_diff, uncommitted_diff, commit_log,
-- truncation flags) is server-fetched on viewer mount and is NOT
-- part of slot state -- two reasons: it is too large for a URL,
-- and it is pure server data that re-fetches cheaply.
value DiffComparator {
    -- Free-form key the server understands. Today: a comparator
    -- string ("HEAD..base", "<sha>..<sha>", etc.). Treated opaquely
    -- by the slot; the diff endpoint owns its grammar.
    key: String         -- non-empty
}

-- Patch provenance for an open prose file. Set when the user opens
-- the file from a patch context (clicking a modified file in a
-- message's patch summary). Drives the auto-scroll-to-first-modified-
-- line affordance and the per-line "modified" highlight. NOT in the
-- URL: cannot encode Set<Integer> sensibly, and re-deriving from the
-- triggering message is cheaper than encoding it. Loss on cold
-- reload is acceptable -- the file still opens, just without the
-- highlights.
value PatchContext {
    modified_lines: Set<Integer>      -- non-empty
    first_modified_line: Integer?     -- target for auto-scroll
}

-- Snapshot of the URL search params describing a viewer slot, in the
-- form they would appear in `?...`. Used as the persistence shape for
-- per-conversation last-viewer storage (REQ-VS-014). Encoded as a
-- string so the storage helper does not need to mirror the URL grammar
-- -- a future `?viewer=diff` shape will round-trip without spec
-- changes here. "Slot was none" is represented by ABSENCE: the
-- implementation removes the localStorage key on close rather than
-- writing an empty string. Both forms (absent key OR empty string,
-- should one ever be written) are equivalent at the read site, which
-- treats them identically as "no restore."
--
-- Example today: "file=%2FUsers%2Fme%2Fproj%2Fsrc%2Ffoo.ts&root=%2FUsers%2Fme%2Fproj"
-- Both `file` and `root` are absolute paths today (current openFile
-- signature). `root` is the conversation's worktree root and is used
-- server-side as a containment gate (the resolved file must live
-- within it) and client-side to render the relative display path.
-- The `?viewer=` prefix is not present in today's URL -- only `file`
-- and `root` -- because the prose-only URL contract pre-dates the
-- unified slot work in REQ-VS-002. Compaction (e.g. encoding `file`
-- as relative-to-root) is in scope for that unification, not here.
value ViewerUrlSnapshot {
    params: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The four kinds of slot state. Encoded in URL as ?viewer=<kind>.
-- `none` corresponds to "no ?viewer= param present."
enum ViewerKind { none | prose | diff | browser }

-- Note: ConversationEntered carries an entry-source discriminator
-- (`cold_reload` vs `in_app`) modelled inline at the event call site
-- below rather than as a top-level enum. `cold_reload` = the SPA just
-- mounted (browser refresh, iOS PWA cold start, shared link click);
-- the URL is authoritative. `in_app` = the user navigated within the
-- SPA (sidebar click, command palette, programmatic navigate); the
-- destination URL is bare unless we restore it from per-conversation
-- storage. The implementation distinguishes these via
-- `useLocation().key` -- documented react-router v6/v7 behavior: the
-- initial location key is `'default'`, every subsequent navigate
-- mints a fresh key.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The viewer slot for one conversation. There is exactly one per
-- active Conversation (conversation row, not browser tab). The
-- slot's state is derived from the URL on every render -- the
-- in-memory representation is a cache of url-driven truth, never
-- a source.
entity ViewerSlot {
    conversation: bedrock/Conversation

    -- Discriminator. The variants below carry the per-kind data.
    kind: ViewerKind

    -- Variant data, scoped to the matching kind by `when` clauses.
    -- These are non-null when the discriminator is set; the type
    -- system enforces they are absent otherwise.
    prose_file: ProseFile when kind = prose
    diff_key:   DiffComparator when kind = diff
    -- browser variant carries no data; conversation.browser_session_active
    -- is the authoritative liveness signal.

    -- patchContext is conversation-scoped, NOT slot-scoped: it
    -- accompanies the prose file when set, but is preserved across
    -- close-and-reopen of the same file within a conversation.
    -- (Today: lives in FileExplorerContext's useScopedState, keyed by
    -- conversation slug.) When the URL hydrates a file path on cold
    -- reload, patchContext is null and the prose reader renders the
    -- file without modified-line highlights. Spec'd as "may be present"
    -- so the rule set covers both cases.
    patch_context: PatchContext? when kind = prose

    -- Slot transitions. Any kind can move to any other kind in one
    -- step (user opens a file while diff was showing, etc.); none is
    -- the start state and the universal close target.
    transitions kind {
        none   -> prose
        none   -> diff
        none   -> browser
        prose  -> none
        prose  -> diff
        prose  -> browser
        diff   -> none
        diff   -> prose
        diff   -> browser
        browser -> none
        browser -> prose
        browser -> diff
    }
}

-- Per-conversation last-viewer storage is modelled via the events
-- below (`ViewerSnapshotPersisted`, `ViewerSnapshotCleared`,
-- `UrlRestorationRequested`) rather than as a typed entity, because
-- Allium does not yet support indexed assignment in `ensures:` clauses.
-- The implementation backs these events with `localStorage` keyed by
-- conversation slug:
--
--   localStorage["phoenix:lastviewer:<slug>"] = ViewerUrlSnapshot.params
--
-- Survives page reload, cleared by explicit close (REQ-VS-004), and
-- cleared when the conversation is hard-deleted (REQ-BED-032 cascade).
-- Keyed by slug rather than conversation id so the read on entry can
-- fire from `useParams<{slug}>` without an extra slug→id lookup.
-- Slugs are stable identifiers.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Events (referenced inline by rules; not declared separately)
------------------------------------------------------------

-- User-driven events: clicking a file row, a View Diff button, the
-- browser launcher, or the close button on the active viewer.
--   UserOpensProse(slot, file, patch?)
--   UserOpensDiff(slot, key)
--   UserOpensBrowser(slot)
--   UserClosesViewer(slot)
--
-- URL-driven event: the URL search params changed (back button,
-- shared link click, programmatic navigate). Distinguished from
-- UserOpens* because it is the same transition without the event
-- having a single user click as its cause -- it might be route-
-- restoration on mount, browser back, or another tab's pushState
-- via storage events. Treating it as one event keeps the rule set
-- tight; the implementation reads the URL once and dispatches.
--   UrlSlotChanged(slot, next_kind, next_prose_file?, next_diff_key?)
--
-- Server-driven events: bedrock's Conversation.browser_session_active
-- transitioned. The slot listens for the rising edge to auto-open
-- (when slot is empty) and the falling edge to auto-close (when
-- slot = browser). See REQ-VS-008 / REQ-VS-009.
--   BrowserSessionActiveRose(slot)
--   BrowserSessionActiveFell(slot)
--
-- Conversation navigation: the user navigated to a different
-- conversation (slug change). The slot resets because URL params
-- naturally don't survive a different /c/:slug path. Captured as
-- an explicit event for clarity.
--   ConversationChanged(slot)
--
-- Conversation entry: the user lands on /c/:slug, either via SPA
-- mount (cold_reload) or in-app navigation (in_app). Distinct from
-- ConversationChanged because it covers the cold-reload entry (no
-- prior conversation to change FROM) and because it carries the
-- entry-source discriminator the restore rule depends on.
--   ConversationEntered(slot, source: ConversationEntrySource)
--
-- Hard-delete cascade: bedrock removed the conversation row server-
-- side. Storage entries for that slug must be cleared so a future
-- conversation that happens to reuse the slug doesn't inherit them.
--   ConversationHardDeleted(slug)

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- ===========================================================
-- VS 1. OPENING A VIEWER (REQ-VS-001, REQ-VS-002, REQ-VS-003)
-- ===========================================================

-- @guidance
--   Opening any viewer is a write-through to the URL: the
--   implementation calls setSearchParams({ viewer, ... }, { replace: true }).
--   `replace` (vs push) keeps the back-button stack clean -- a user
--   clicking through five files shouldn't accumulate five history
--   entries. The slot's kind transition is computed from the URL on
--   the next render, NOT mutated directly.
rule UserOpensProseFile {
    when: UserOpensProse(slot, file, patch?)
    requires: not slot.conversation.is_terminal  -- no point opening a viewer on a terminal conversation; UI gates this too
    ensures: slot.kind = prose
    ensures: slot.prose_file = file
    ensures: slot.patch_context = patch
    -- Implicitly clears diff_key: the type system enforces
    -- diff_key absent when kind != diff.
}

rule UserOpensDiffComparator {
    when: UserOpensDiff(slot, key)
    requires: not slot.conversation.is_terminal
    ensures: slot.kind = diff
    ensures: slot.diff_key = key
}

rule UserOpensBrowserViewer {
    when: UserOpensBrowser(slot)
    requires: slot.conversation.browser_session_active
    -- The browser launcher button is gated on browser_session_active
    -- in the UI; the spec rule reflects that gate. A user "opening"
    -- the browser without an active session is a UI bug, not a
    -- transition we need to model.
    ensures: slot.kind = browser
}

-- ===========================================================
-- VS 2. CLOSING A VIEWER (REQ-VS-004)
-- ===========================================================

rule UserClosesActiveViewer {
    when: UserClosesViewer(slot)
    requires: slot.kind != none
    ensures: slot.kind = none
    -- Variant data (prose_file, diff_key) is structurally cleared
    -- by the kind transition.
}

-- ===========================================================
-- VS 3. URL HYDRATION (REQ-VS-005, REQ-VS-006)
-- ===========================================================

-- @guidance
--   These rules describe what happens when the URL is the trigger,
--   not user click: page mount with non-empty search params (cold
--   reload), browser back/forward, or a shared link being followed.
--   The implementation listens to react-router's useSearchParams;
--   the spec sees only the resulting transition.
--
--   patch_context is NOT in the URL -- on URL-driven hydration of
--   prose, it is null. The prose reader still mounts; the file
--   loads; the user just doesn't see the modified-line highlights.
--   This is the correct trade for cold-reload restoration: keep
--   identity (file path), drop ephemeral provenance (which lines
--   were modified by the message that opened it).
rule UrlHydratesNone {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, none, _, _)
    ensures: slot.kind = none
}

rule UrlHydratesProse {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, prose, file, _)
    requires: file != null
    ensures: slot.kind = prose
    ensures: slot.prose_file = file
    ensures: slot.patch_context = null
}

rule UrlHydratesDiff {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, diff, _, key)
    requires: key != null
    ensures: slot.kind = diff
    ensures: slot.diff_key = key
}

rule UrlHydratesBrowser {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, browser, _, _)
    -- No requires on browser_session_active here: the URL might
    -- restore ?viewer=browser after the session has died. The viewer
    -- mount path renders an "ended" state when active=false; spec'd
    -- as: the slot kind reflects URL truth even if the underlying
    -- session is gone. The auto-close rule (REQ-VS-009) catches the
    -- subsequent server signal.
    ensures: slot.kind = browser
}

-- Defensive: the URL has ?viewer=prose but no file param (or vice
-- versa). The implementation should normalize this away (treat as
-- ?viewer=none), and the spec asserts this normalization rule for
-- correctness.
rule UrlMalformedNormalizesToNone {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, kind, file, key)
    requires: (kind = prose and file = null)
              or (kind = diff and key = null)
    ensures: slot.kind = none
    ensures: UrlNormalizationRequested(slot)  -- triggers a setSearchParams clearing the bogus params
}

-- ===========================================================
-- VS 4. BROWSER SESSION EDGE TRIGGERS (REQ-VS-008, REQ-VS-009)
-- ===========================================================

-- @guidance
--   browser_session_active is server-authoritative and lives on the
--   Conversation entity (see bedrock.allium). Rising edge means a
--   tool just spawned a browser session for this conversation;
--   surface it. Falling edge means the session died (kill, idle
--   cleanup); don't leave the user staring at a stale "no browser
--   yet" overlay.
--
--   IMPORTANT: this is a ONE-WAY auto-open on the rising edge ONLY.
--   A cold reload that mounts with browser_session_active = true
--   does NOT auto-open the browser viewer; the spec relies on the
--   URL being authoritative for that case. If the user wants the
--   browser viewer back after a reload, the URL must say so. The
--   ConversationPage launcher button is the manual entry point.
rule BrowserSessionRoseAutoOpensSlot {
    when: BrowserSessionActiveRose(slot)
    requires: slot.kind = none
    ensures: slot.kind = browser
}

-- @guidance: when the rising edge fires while slot.kind != none,
-- no rule applies -- the user has prose or diff open and we do
-- not steal their viewer. The launcher chip (rendered by
-- ConversationPage when browser_session_active is true) is the
-- manual entry point.

rule BrowserSessionFellAutoClosesSlot {
    when: BrowserSessionActiveFell(slot)
    requires: slot.kind = browser
    ensures: slot.kind = none
}

-- @guidance: when the falling edge fires while slot.kind != browser,
-- no rule applies -- the slot doesn't change. The launcher chip
-- naturally disappears because it's driven by
-- conversation.browser_session_active, not by slot.kind.

-- ===========================================================
-- VS 5. CONVERSATION SWITCH (REQ-VS-010)
-- ===========================================================

-- @guidance
--   When the user navigates from /c/A to /c/B, the URL path changes;
--   search params are NOT carried (react-router's navigate() drops
--   them by default). Even if they were, conversation B's slot
--   should not inherit conversation A's viewer -- a file path from
--   A's worktree won't resolve under B's worktree. The spec captures
--   this as an explicit reset.
--
--   patch_context, which is conversation-scoped state (NOT URL-
--   driven), is also cleared on conversation change. The
--   FileExplorerProvider's useScopedState handles this in the
--   implementation.
rule ConversationChangeResetsSlot {
    when: ConversationChanged(slot)
    ensures: slot.kind = none
}

-- ===========================================================
-- VS 5b. PER-CONVERSATION VIEWER PERSISTENCE (REQ-VS-014)
-- ===========================================================

-- @guidance
--   REQ-VS-010 still holds: navigating from /c/A to /c/B never
--   carries A's viewer state INTO B. This section is the symmetric
--   case: when the user navigates back TO a conversation they
--   previously had a viewer open in, restore that viewer.
--
--   Two design decisions are baked in:
--
--   (D1) Cold reload does NOT restore. A user who reloaded the tab
--        with a bare URL gets a bare URL back. Only in-app navigation
--        triggers restore. This keeps the URL the unambiguous source
--        of truth on cold reload (REQ-VS-005, REQ-VS-006) and avoids
--        surprising a user who deliberately shared `/c/A` (no viewer
--        params) with their team only to have it auto-open a file
--        the recipient cannot see.
--
--   (D2) Storage is localStorage (matches the per-file scroll-restore
--        tier in ProseReader). Survives reload but is per-tab-group;
--        cross-tab races are best-effort last-writer-wins. Acceptable
--        for v1 -- same semantics as the scroll restore.
--
--   The persistence layer sits BELOW the URL but ABOVE the slot:
--   in-app navigation reads storage and decorates the URL before
--   commit; the slot rules then hydrate from the URL exactly as
--   they would for a cold reload that happened to have params. No
--   slot rule changes; only entry-time URL synthesis.

-- Storage write: every event that lands the slot in a non-none kind
-- (user-driven open OR URL-driven hydration) persists the current URL
-- params for this conversation's slug. Modelled as one rule per
-- triggering event because Allium triggers fire on events, not on
-- entity-field transitions. Subsumes both "user just opened prose"
-- and "URL hydrated prose on cold reload" -- both leave a storage
-- entry so a subsequent in-app navigation away and back restores
-- the same viewer.
--
-- @guidance: the implementation can collapse these into a single
-- effect that watches `(slot.kind, current URL params)` and writes
-- on any transition where `slot.kind != none`. The per-event
-- decomposition here is for spec analysability; it is not a
-- prescription on how to factor the React effects.

rule UserOpensProseWritesStorage {
    when: UserOpensProse(slot, file, patch?)
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotPersisted(slot)
}

rule UserOpensDiffWritesStorage {
    when: UserOpensDiff(slot, key)
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotPersisted(slot)
}

rule UserOpensBrowserWritesStorage {
    when: UserOpensBrowser(slot)
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotPersisted(slot)
}

rule UrlHydratesProseWritesStorage {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, prose, file, _)
    requires: file != null
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotPersisted(slot)
}

rule UrlHydratesDiffWritesStorage {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, diff, _, key)
    requires: key != null
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotPersisted(slot)
}

rule UrlHydratesBrowserWritesStorage {
    when: UrlSlotChanged(slot, browser, _, _)
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotPersisted(slot)
}

-- Storage clear on explicit close. Without this, clicking X to close
-- a file then leaving the conversation and returning would silently
-- re-open the file -- undoing the user's close intent. Co-fires with
-- UserClosesActiveViewer (slot.kind transitions to none); listed
-- separately for clarity. The cleared key is the conversation's
-- slug -- storage is slug-keyed throughout (see ConversationHardDeleted
-- event for the parallel cascade path).
rule UserClosesClearsStorage {
    when: UserClosesViewer(slot)
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotCleared(slot.conversation.slug)
}

-- The restore rule. Fires only on in-app entry to a conversation
-- whose URL has no viewer params. Implementation reads
-- localStorage["phoenix:lastviewer:<slug>"] when handling the
-- emitted UrlRestorationRequested event; if absent or empty, the
-- effect is a no-op and the slot stays none. Modelling the
-- localStorage read as a `requires:` would tie the spec to
-- implementation; emitting the event unconditionally and letting the
-- handler short-circuit on empty storage keeps the slot rules pure.
rule InAppNavigationRestoresFromStorage {
    when: ConversationEntered(slot, in_app)
    requires: slot.kind = none
    ensures: UrlRestorationRequested(slot)
    -- Effect: handler reads storage, calls setSearchParams(stored,
    -- { replace: true }) if any. On the next render the existing
    -- UrlHydratesProse / UrlHydratesDiff / UrlHydratesBrowser rules
    -- fire and the slot reaches the restored kind. Two render
    -- cycles: this rule emits the request, URL update fires the
    -- hydration rule, slot updates.
}

-- @guidance: cold-reload entry is intentionally NOT covered by a
-- restore rule. The URL is authoritative -- if it has params, the
-- existing UrlHydrates* rules apply; if bare, the slot stays none.
-- Storage is consulted only on in-app entry. This is decision D1.

-- Storage cleanup on hard-delete. Without this, slug entries
-- accumulate forever for conversations the user has explicitly
-- removed. The slug-keyed storage assumes slugs are stable
-- identifiers; if a future server change recycles slugs, an
-- orphan entry would silently restore a file under the wrong
-- conversation.
rule HardDeleteClearsStorage {
    when: ConversationHardDeleted(slug)
    ensures: ViewerSnapshotCleared(slug)
}

-- ===========================================================
-- VS 6. PROSE PATCH CONTEXT (REQ-VS-011)
-- ===========================================================

-- @guidance
--   Opening a file with a patch context vs without: same kind=prose
--   transition; the patch_context field captures the difference.
--   Re-opening the same file with a NEW patch context (e.g. user
--   clicks a different message that touched the same file) replaces
--   the previous patch_context atomically.
--
--   On URL-driven hydration of prose, patch_context is null (see
--   UrlHydratesProse). It can only be populated by a UserOpensProse
--   event that carries one.
rule ProseOpenWithPatchContext {
    when: UserOpensProse(slot, file, patch)
    requires: patch != null
    ensures: slot.kind = prose
    ensures: slot.prose_file = file
    ensures: slot.patch_context = patch
}

rule ProseOpenWithoutPatchContext {
    when: UserOpensProse(slot, file, patch?)
    requires: patch = null
    ensures: slot.kind = prose
    ensures: slot.prose_file = file
    ensures: slot.patch_context = null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The user surface for the viewer slot. Reuses bedrock/User as the
-- actor because the viewer is a feature of the same authenticated
-- conversation experience -- not a separate persona.
--
-- This surface declares the user-driven events; URL-driven and
-- server-driven events (UrlSlotChanged, BrowserSessionActiveRose,
-- BrowserSessionActiveFell, ConversationChanged) are implicit, the
-- same way bedrock leaves ServerRestarted implicit -- they are
-- system signals, not user actions.
surface ConversationViewer {
    facing user: bedrock/User

    context slot: ViewerSlot

    exposes:
        slot.kind
        slot.prose_file when slot.kind = prose
        slot.diff_key when slot.kind = diff

    provides:
        UserOpensProse(slot, file, patch?)
            when not slot.conversation.is_terminal
        UserOpensDiff(slot, key)
            when not slot.conversation.is_terminal
        UserOpensBrowser(slot)
            when slot.conversation.browser_session_active
        UserClosesViewer(slot)
            when slot.kind != none
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- @invariant VariantDataPresence
--   prose_file is set iff kind = prose; diff_key is set iff kind =
--   diff. The `when kind = ...` annotations on the entity fields
--   are the structural enforcement; this invariant restates the
--   contract for spec consumers (and gives `allium analyse` a
--   target to verify against the rule set).
invariant ProseDataPresentIffProseKind {
    for s in ViewerSlot:
        (s.kind = prose) implies (s.prose_file != null)
}

invariant ProseDataAbsentWhenNotProseKind {
    for s in ViewerSlot:
        (s.kind != prose) implies (s.prose_file = null)
}

invariant DiffDataPresentIffDiffKind {
    for s in ViewerSlot:
        (s.kind = diff) implies (s.diff_key != null)
}

invariant DiffDataAbsentWhenNotDiffKind {
    for s in ViewerSlot:
        (s.kind != diff) implies (s.diff_key = null)
}

-- @invariant PatchContextOnlyDuringProse
--   patch_context can only be populated when kind = prose. Otherwise
--   it is null. (The `when kind = prose` annotation enforces this
--   structurally.)
invariant PatchContextOnlyDuringProse {
    for s in ViewerSlot:
        (s.patch_context != null) implies (s.kind = prose)
}

-- SingleSlotKind is enforced structurally by the enum -- kind is
-- a single field with values in { none, prose, diff, browser }.
-- An invariant restating this would be tautological.

-- UrlIsSourceOfTruth is documented in the entity comment and the
-- @guidance blocks on the rules; it is an implementation contract
-- (URL → state derivation runs every render) rather than a model
-- invariant Allium can check, so it lives in prose. The
-- corresponding test obligation: every transition that changes
-- slot.kind has a sibling test that asserts the URL matches.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Implementation Notes (non-normative)
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Today's implementation has the slot SPLIT across three sources of
-- truth:
--   - prose: URL via FileExplorerProvider (REQ-VS land in PR #47)
--   - diff:  scoped React state via DiffViewerStateProvider
--   - browser: scoped React state via BrowserViewStateProvider
--
-- The mutex rules (UserOpens* sets one and clears the others) are
-- enforced by useEffects in ConversationPage.tsx -- three separate
-- effects watching for the "two are open" condition and closing the
-- losing one. This is what makes the current implementation fragile:
-- adding a fourth viewer or changing the priority order requires
-- editing N effects, and the "no two open at once" rule lives in
-- imperative code rather than the type system.
--
-- The unified-slot work (task TBD) collapses these three providers
-- into one. The state shape becomes the discriminated union spec'd
-- above; the URL contract widens to ?viewer= covering all three
-- kinds; the mutex effects in ConversationPage.tsx are deleted
-- (replaced by the type system); and diff and browser join prose in
-- surviving cold reload.
--
-- Test coverage for the migration: every transition in `transitions
-- kind` should have a corresponding vitest case driving the URL and
-- asserting the slot. The bedrock.allium spec's `transitions
-- core_status` list serves the same generative purpose for the
-- backend reducer.
